# NOETHERVIM: RUNNING CODE AND TERMINALS

Four ways to run what you are editing, and the list that collects what went
wrong. Most of this is core; two sections name the bundle they need.

Table of contents:

- [The terminal](*terminal*)
- [Running the file you are in](*run*)
- [File or project](*scope*)
- [The quickfix list](*quickfix*)
- [Task runners and REPLs](*bundles*)
- [Recap](*recap*)

## THE TERMINAL *terminal*

`<C-w>t` toggles a terminal along the bottom of the window and drops you
into it ready to type.

It reuses the same terminal each time rather than opening a new one, so the
shell keeps its history and its working directory between toggles.

`<Esc><Esc>` leaves terminal mode and gives the keys back to Neovim, so you
can scroll the output, search it, and yank from it like any other buffer.
A single `<Esc>` goes to the shell, which is what you want when the program
running inside it expects one.

This replaces Vim's `<C-w>t` ("go to the top window"); `1<C-w>w` still does
that.

