## RUNNING THE FILE YOU ARE IN *run*

    `<Leader>rf`   run this file
    `<Leader>rp`   run the project around it
    `<Leader>rc`   run this file in a floating window
    `<Leader>rT`   send the run command to a named terminal

What each language runs is one table, so all four agree about what "run"
means for a given filetype.

`rc` and `rT` are core. `rf` and `rp` need the `task-runner` bundle, and
`rT` also needs `better-term`.

Version managers are honored. The interpreter is resolved through mise,
asdf, pyenv, rbenv, nodenv or goenv for the buffer's own directory before
anything runs, so a project pinned to an older Python gets that one rather
than whichever happens to be first on your PATH.

## FILE OR PROJECT *scope*

The difference between `rf` and `rp` is the project around the file.

In a Cargo crate, `rf` runs `cargo run`; on a loose `.rs` file with no
project, it compiles that one file. `rp` always runs the project's own
entry point, found from `Cargo.toml`, `go.mod`, `package.json`, a Maven
file or a makefile, and does nothing when there is no project around the
buffer.

Interpreted languages have `rf` only, since running the file and running
the project are the same act.

## THE QUICKFIX LIST *quickfix*

When a build or a search produces a list of places to visit, it lands in
the quickfix list.

    `<C-w><C-q>`   open the list
    `<Leader>q`    toggle it
    `]q` `[q`      next and previous entry
    `]Q` `[Q`      last and first entry

Inside the list, `<CR>` jumps to an entry and leaves the list open;
`<S-CR>` jumps and closes it. `q` closes it, since it is a list you read
rather than type into.

The bracket pair here is the same grammar as everywhere else: `[` back, `]`
forward, over whatever kind of list you named. That is why moving through
build errors uses the same two keys as moving through buffers.

